// Split out of ./gc.bit (#1503): the address->object membership index.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The address->object membership index
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `owns` is the mark hot path: it runs once per traced reference. A linear walk
// of the all-objects list there makes tracing O(objects^2) — that was a real,
// measured regression in this codebase (fixed in 3dc87a3, ~9x on
// the h2 download path), so the index is ported, not skipped.
//
// Open addressing with linear probing over a power-of-two table of body
// addresses. A general-purpose hash map would do; the internal layout is
// NOT part of any contract (the index is a pure speed structure over the
// authoritative all-objects list), so a simpler scheme is free to differ as
// long as `owns` answers identically. It does: the index mirrors exactly the
// insert/remove points that mutate `all`.
//
// Backed by its OWN reserve, never the object heap — the
// `index_alloc = std.heap.page_allocator`. Index bytes must not count toward
// `liveBytes`, or they would perturb the growth trigger.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PLANNED REPLACEMENT (#3147): a per-span allocated-slot bitmap
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// #3147 measured this table at 24 bytes per slot at peak capacity (three
// tables resident at once — live, spare, and every smaller table ever
// abandoned into the bump-only `gcIdxRes`): 30% of the `alloc` bench's RSS,
// 208 MiB on `json`. This is #3175's design for the fix — DESIGN ONLY, no
// code below this comment changed. #3176 adds the bitmap write-only, #3177
// rewires `gcOwns` to read it, #3178 stops `idxInsert`/`idxRemove` for
// span-backed objects, #3179 unmaps what is left of `gcIdxRes`.
//
// 1. WHERE IT LIVES: AN EXTENSION OF THE SPAN HEADER, PER CLASS, NOT A TABLE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Since #2374 a span (`runtime/alloc/alloc.bit`) is self-describing and
// aligned to its own size (`allocSpanBytes` = 131072, `allocSpanOf` a mask),
// so the bitmap belongs INSIDE the span it describes: immediately after the
// existing 10 fixed header words (`allocSpanHdrWords`, alloc.bit:244) and
// before the first carved slot — an extension of the reserved PREFIX
// `spanSkip` already computes (alloc.bit:269-273: "Slots ... the header
// consumes at the span base. Rounded UP to a whole slot"), not a change to
// the fixed 10-word block's own offsets (spanClass..spanEmpty keep their
// exact positions).
//
// NOT a side table: a side table reintroduces exactly the disease this epic
// is curing — a structure that must grow, rehash and outlive what it
// describes, when the span already needs no such table for its OWN
// bookkeeping (alloc.bit:9-12: "no address->span lookup table ... a span is
// aligned to its own size"). Embedding it also means the bitmap needs no
// reclaim step of its own — see the #3179 note below.
//
// SIZING, AVOIDING THE CIRCULARITY. The bitmap must hold at least
// `allocSpanSlots(idx)` bits, but that count already SUBTRACTS the skip the
// bitmap is part of, so sizing off the final slot count is circular. Size it
// instead off the slot count BEFORE any prefix is removed — `naive(idx) =
// allocSpanBytes / allocClassSize(idx)` — a safe upper bound
// (`allocSpanSlots(idx) < naive(idx)` always, removing a nonzero prefix only
// shrinks the usable count): `bitmapWords(idx) = (naive(idx) + 63) / 64`.
// The reserved prefix becomes `ceil((allocSpanHdrWords*8 + bitmapWords(idx)*8)
// / cs)` slots — the same "round up to a whole slot" shape `spanSkip` already
// has, fed a larger byte count. Comptime per class, so it costs nothing at
// carve time beyond what `spanSkip` already costs.
//
// COST: worst case is class 0 (8-byte slots, `naive` = 16384) at 256 words =
// 2048 bytes, ~1.6% of a 131072-byte span. Every larger class costs
// proportionally less. This is a FIXED, comptime, per-class overhead —
// unlike the table's `24 x cap`, it does not scale with how many objects the
// GC trigger has let accumulate.
//
// THIS DOES NOT WIDEN `gcWords` (38) OR `heapWords` (41) — both are
// unrelated word-block sizes (the `Gc` struct, the heap-state block); this
// changes only `allocSpanHdrWords`'s per-class SKIP, which already varies by
// class today (`spanSkip(cs)`) and is not one of the two boot-pinned totals.
//
// 2. THE SEMANTICS gcOwns MUST MATCH, BIT FOR BIT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Today (gcalloc.bit:174-193): `gcOwns(g, body)` is `idxContains` — or, index
// disabled, `gcOwnsLinear`, the oracle — and `gcOwnsLinear` answers true iff
// `body` equals `bodyFromHeader(o)` for some `o` CURRENTLY LINKED on `gcAll`.
// "Currently linked" is populated at exactly two call sites: `linkObject`
// (gcalloc.bit:23-35, inserts) and `sweep` + `freeObject` (gcmark.bit:142-179,
// unlinks then removes). The cases the ticket asks for, matched exactly by
// list membership today:
//   - EXACT BASE of a linked object: true (the list holds exactly this).
//   - INTERIOR (any nonzero offset into a live object's body): the list
//     holds unique object bases, so an interior address never equals one —
//     false, with no special-casing in the linear scan.
//   - FREED (post-sweep, back on the allocator's free list): unlinked from
//     `gcAll` before `freeObject` runs — false.
//   - FOREIGN (never came from this heap): never linked — false.
//
// A DIVISION-BASED BITMAP LOOKUP DOES NOT GET "INTERIOR" RIGHT FOR FREE, AND
// THAT IS THE SHARPEST CORRECTNESS POINT HERE. Given a candidate `ref`, the
// arithmetic is `raw = ref - gcHeaderSize` (gc.bit:79, the header sits before
// the body), `span = allocSpanOf(raw)`, `carveStart = span + spanSkip*cs`
// (the address of slot 0), `n = (raw - carveStart) / cs` (floored). Floor
// division maps EVERY address inside a slot's `cs` bytes to the SAME `n` as
// the slot's own base — an interior `ref` and its slot's base alias to one
// bit, so the bit alone cannot tell them apart. The base check must be
// separate and explicit — `(raw - carveStart) % cs == 0` AND `0 <= n <
// spanTotal` — BEFORE the bitmap is consulted; only then does testing bit
// `n` answer the "exact base" question `gcOwnsLinear`'s exact-equality scan
// answers for free. Skipping the remainder check is a live false positive —
// "scan garbage as if it were a pointer" — for every nonzero offset into a
// live object's body a conservative root scan (ABI.md §5) or a
// reference-array trace (gcmark.bit:74-85) could present.
//
// THE BIT MUST BE SET/CLEARED AT THE SAME CALL SITES `idxInsert`/`idxRemove`
// ARE TODAY — `linkObject`/`freeObject` — NOT inside `runtime/alloc/`'s
// `heapTakeSmallLocked`/`heapGiveSmallLocked`. This corrects how #3176 was
// scoped, not a preference: `gcHeapAlloc`/`gcHeapFree` have a THIRD caller
// besides `gcAlloc`/`gcAllocRaw` — `gcInit`'s mark worklist (gc.bit:202-274,
// freed at gc.bit:506-509) — which allocates and frees straight through the
// heap WITHOUT ever calling `linkObject`, exactly so the worklist is never
// treated as a traced object. `gcOwns` on the worklist's own body address is
// FALSE today (it is never linked), and that address IS reachable to
// `gcMarkRoot` in practice — `noteBody` (gcalloc.bit:10-17) is skipped for it
// too, so `[gcLoAddr, gcHiAddr)` does not exclude it once other objects in
// the same span widen the bounds around it. A bitmap set at ALLOCATOR
// take-time (what `heapTakeSmallLocked`/`heapGiveSmallLocked` see) cannot
// make this distinction: `runtime/alloc/` is deliberately GC-oblivious
// (module header: "no imports at all", "this file compiles unchanged for
// every target") and has no way to know a given take is the worklist rather
// than a real object. Setting the bit at `linkObject`/`freeObject` instead —
// which already have exactly the "is this a real, tracked object" context
// `idxInsert`/`idxRemove` use — keeps the worklist's slot bit permanently 0,
// matching `gcOwnsLinear` exactly. `runtime/alloc/` should only be asked for
// the pure addressing primitive (span + slot index from an address,
// mirroring `allocSpanOf`); the DECISION of when to flip a bit stays in
// `runtime/gc/`, the same layering `idxInsert`/`idxRemove` already have.
//
// THE TIMING GAP THIS OPENS IS SAFE, AND WHY, EXPLICITLY, SO IT IS NOT
// "FIXED" LATER BY MOVING A SAFEPOINT INTO IT: `linkObject` runs strictly
// after `gcHeapAlloc` returns (gcalloc.bit:129-142), so between a slot
// leaving the allocator's free list and the bit actually being set there is
// a window with no GC-visible record of the object at all — the SAME window
// that exists today between `heapTakeSmallLocked` returning and `idxInsert`
// running. It is unobservable by a concurrent mark pass only because nothing
// in that window is `@nosplit`-legal to interrupt at a safepoint (§10.3) and
// `registerAllocator` (gcalloc.bit:54-84) has already registered this thread,
// so a stop rendezvous BLOCKS on it rather than proceeding without a stack
// map. Do not add a safepoint poll between `gcHeapAlloc` and `linkObject`
// without re-deriving this.
//
// 3. LARGE OBJECTS: NOT SPAN-BACKED, SO THE HASH INDEX SURVIVES FOR THEM
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `heapLargeAllocLocked`/`heapLargeFreeLocked` (alloc.bit:734-747) carry NO
// per-object metadata at all — pure byte counters over a direct
// `memMapPages` region (gc.bit:386-399). There is no span to mask to and
// nowhere to embed a bitmap; `allocClassify` returns -1 for exactly this
// population (classify.bit:74: `needed > allocMaxSmall`, i.e. > 16384 bytes,
// or an alignment wider than a page). So the bitmap answers ONLY span-backed
// addresses; a large object's `gcOwns` must keep going through
// `idxContains`/`gcOwnsLinear` exactly as today, scoped down to large
// objects once #3178 stops calling `idxInsert`/`idxRemove` for span-backed
// ones. THE HASH INDEX DOES NOT RETIRE. #3177 must dispatch on
// span-backed-ness (an owning span found) before falling to the hash path,
// and #3178's `idxInsert`/`idxRemove` removal is scoped to that same
// span-backed case only.
//
// #3178'S "idxbytes=0" TARGET, AS WRITTEN, IS NOT REACHABLE ON
// `bench/cases/alloc/alloc.bit`, AND THE TICKET'S OWN PREMISE THAT IT HAS "no
// large allocations" IS WRONG (flagged there directly, #3175's comment, not
// a silent edit). Its `batch` slice grows by doubling
// (`runtime/root/slices.bit:218`, `newcap = vcap * 2`) from 1 toward `per` =
// 5000 backing WORDS — every slice, including `[]Node`, stores one 8-byte
// handle per element regardless of element width, and `Node` is itself
// boxed, a separate 48-byte class-3 object (`fieldLayoutFor`,
// lowercall.bit:406-441: "string/slice/map/chan/tuple/struct/... are 8-byte
// handles"). The doubling sequence crosses `allocMaxSmall` (16384 bytes =
// 2048 words) once length passes 2048, so for roughly the back half of every
// 5000-element batch the LIVE `batch` backing buffer is a large,
// direct-mapped allocation — 2000 times over the run, one per batch. Worse,
// `reserveGrow`'s minimum unit is a full `reserveChunkBytes` = 4194304
// (mem.bit:299,351) regardless of how small the request is, so the FIRST
// large allocation of the run maps one whole 4 MiB chunk that a bump reserve
// (`gcIdxRes`, no free list) never releases on its own — which is exactly
// today's baseline (#3146: `idxchunks=1 idxbytes=4194304`) and will very
// likely read UNCHANGED immediately after #3178, not zero. Only #3179's
// ownership-walk unmap can move that number, and only once the chunk holds
// no live large entry at unmap time. #3178 should restate its acceptance
// around a MEASURED post-fix number — expect it to stay near 4194304 until
// #3179 lands, not to reach zero at #3178 alone.
//
// WHAT THIS MEANS FOR #3179's OWNERSHIP WALK: the bitmap itself adds NO new
// reclaim work — it dies with its span under the EXISTING `spanOwned`-gated
// path (#2375, `heapReclaimSpansLocked`), unchanged by any of this. #3179's
// walk stays scoped to `gcIdxRes`'s own chunks exactly as filed, but per the
// paragraph above it must not assume `gcIdxRes` is retirable wholesale after
// #3178 — the shrunk, large-object-only table is still LIVE in it, so the
// walk must distinguish "an orphaned chunk" from "the chunk the live (now
// much smaller) table still occupies," the same distinction `spanOwned`
// already draws for spans.

// splitmix64's finalizer. Computed in `u64` because it relies on modular
// multiplication; `i64` overflow only wraps in v0.1 -- a debug build mode
// that traps instead is specified but not yet implemented (§11.1, #3076).
@nosplit fn idxHash(addr: int, ncap: int): int {
  let x = u64(addr)
  x = x ^ (x >> 30)
  x = x * 13787848793156543929
  x = x ^ (x >> 27)
  x = x * 10723151780598845931
  x = x ^ (x >> 31)
  let mask = u64(ncap - 1)
  return int(x & mask)
}

@nosplit fn idxSlotAddr(table: int, i: int): int {
  return table + i * 8
}

// True if `addr` is present. Probes until an empty slot, which terminates
// because the load factor is held below 100%. Bounded by `ncap` regardless.
//
// THE SENTINEL GUARD IS ABOUT THE QUERY, NOT THE ENTRIES, and that distinction
// is the whole bug it fixes (found by #3177's enumerated differential, present
// on every build before it). `idxEmpty`/`idxTombstone`'s own comment argues
// that 0 and 1 "cannot collide with a real entry" — true, and irrelevant here:
// `idxContains` is asked about ARBITRARY integers, not about entries. A query
// for 1 walks the probe chain comparing `v == addr`, and the first TOMBSTONE it
// meets stores exactly 1 — so `gcOwns(g, 1)` answered TRUE against a table that
// had ever had a removal, disagreeing with `gcOwnsLinear`. `gcMarkRoot`'s
// `[gcLoAddr, gcHiAddr)` gate hides it from the mark path, but
// `runtime/root/iface.bit`'s `dynTypeOf` calls `gcOwns` with no bound at all
// and would go on to read a header at `1 - gcHeaderSize`.
//
// Rejecting both values up front is exact rather than conservative: a real key
// is a body or span address, 8-aligned and non-zero, so neither can be one.
@nosplit fn idxContains(g: *i64, addr: int): bool {
  let ncap = *(g + gcIndexCap)
  if (ncap == 0 || addr == idxTombstone) {
    return false
  }
  let table = *(g + gcIndexAddr)
  let i = idxHash(addr, ncap)
  let probes = 0
  while (probes < ncap) {
    let v = loadWord(idxSlotAddr(table, i))
    if (v == idxEmpty) {
      return false
    }
    if (v == addr) {
      return true
    }
    i = (i + 1) % ncap
    probes = probes + 1
  }
  return false
}

// Place `addr` into `table` assuming capacity is available and `addr` is
// absent. Used by both insert and rehash.
@nosplit fn idxPlace(table: int, ncap: int, addr: int): bool {
  let i = idxHash(addr, ncap)
  let probes = 0
  while (probes < ncap) {
    let slotAddr = idxSlotAddr(table, i)
    let v = loadWord(slotAddr)
    if (v == idxEmpty || v == idxTombstone) {
      storeWord(slotAddr, addr)
      return true
    }
    if (v == addr) {
      return true
    }
    i = (i + 1) % ncap
    probes = probes + 1
  }
  return false
}

// Disable the index permanently and release its table. `owns` then reverts to
// the exact `gcOwnsLinear` scan, so a failure costs speed, never correctness —
// this is the "index unusable, fall back to the list walk" path.
@nosplit fn idxDisable(g: *i64) {
  *(g + gcIndexOk) = 0
  *(g + gcIndexAddr) = 0
  *(g + gcIndexCap) = 0
  *(g + gcIndexLive) = 0
  *(g + gcIndexUsed) = 0
}

// Grow (or first-allocate) the table so one more entry fits under the load
// factor, rehashing the live entries and dropping tombstones.
@nosplit fn idxGrow(g: *i64): bool {
  let oldTable = *(g + gcIndexAddr)
  let oldCap = *(g + gcIndexCap)
  let live = *(g + gcIndexLive)

  // FLOOR AT `oldCap` (#2922). `idxRemove` leaves a tombstone without
  // decrementing `gcIndexUsed`, so right after a big sweep `used` is still
  // high (triggering this rehash on the next insert) while `live` has
  // crashed to the survivor count. Sizing purely off `live` shrank the table
  // to `idxMinCap` and abandoned the old, large one into the bump-only
  // `gcIdxRes` forever, forcing a full doubling regrowth every collection —
  // unbounded RSS growth with a flat `liveBytes`. Capacity must only grow.
  let newCap = idxMinCap
  if (oldCap > newCap) {
    newCap = oldCap
  }
  // Bounded: doubles until the load factor admits `live + 1`; `live` is bounded
  // by the number of live objects, so this terminates in <= 63 iterations.
  let guard = 0
  while (guard < 64) {
    if ((live + 1) * 100 < newCap * idxLoadPct) {
      break
    }
    newCap = newCap * 2
    guard = guard + 1
  }

  // Reuse the spare when it is the right capacity. This is what keeps the
  // index reserve from draining: removals leave TOMBSTONES, which count toward
  // the load factor, so a long alloc/free workload rehashes repeatedly at an
  // UNCHANGED capacity just to clear them. Taking fresh storage each time would
  // exhaust any reserve and permanently disable the index — which would drop
  // `owns` back to the O(objects) linear scan and make tracing O(n^2), the exact
  // regression 3dc87a3 fixed. It is avoided by giving the table
  // back to `page_allocator`; a bump reserve cannot, so one slot is cached.
  // Capacity changes are O(log n) over a whole run, so at most that many tables
  // are ever abandoned.
  let newTable = 0
  if (*(g + gcIdxSpareCap) == newCap) {
    newTable = *(g + gcIdxSpareAddr)
    *(g + gcIdxSpareAddr) = 0
    *(g + gcIdxSpareCap) = 0
  } else {
    // `reserveGrow`, not `reserveTake`: the index scales with the live object
    // count, so a fixed reserve silently disables it on a large heap and drops
    // `owns` back to the O(objects) linear scan (#1638).
    newTable = reserveGrow(g + gcIdxRes, newCap * 8)
  }
  if (newTable == 0) {
    idxDisable(g)
    return false
  }
  // Zero the fresh table: every slot must read `idxEmpty`.
  let i = 0
  while (i < newCap) {
    storeWord(idxSlotAddr(newTable, i), idxEmpty)
    i = i + 1
  }

  // Rehash live entries. Bounded by `oldCap`.
  let moved = 0
  let j = 0
  while (j < oldCap) {
    let v = loadWord(idxSlotAddr(oldTable, j))
    if (v != idxEmpty && v != idxTombstone) {
      if (!idxPlace(newTable, newCap, v)) {
        // Cannot happen: the new table was sized for `live + 1`. Treat as an
        // index failure rather than silently losing a membership entry.
        idxDisable(g)
        return false
      }
      moved = moved + 1
    }
    j = j + 1
  }

  // Park the old table as the spare rather than abandoning it.
  if (oldTable != 0) {
    *(g + gcIdxSpareAddr) = oldTable
    *(g + gcIdxSpareCap) = oldCap
  }
  *(g + gcIndexAddr) = newTable
  *(g + gcIndexCap) = newCap
  *(g + gcIndexLive) = moved
  *(g + gcIndexUsed) = moved
  return true
}

// Record `body` in the index, growing first if the load factor demands it.
@nosplit fn idxInsert(g: *i64, body: int) {
  if (*(g + gcIndexOk) == 0) {
    return
  }
  let ncap = *(g + gcIndexCap)
  let used = *(g + gcIndexUsed)
  if (ncap == 0 || (used + 1) * 100 >= ncap * idxLoadPct) {
    if (!idxGrow(g)) {
      return
    }
    ncap = *(g + gcIndexCap)
  }
  let table = *(g + gcIndexAddr)
  if (!idxPlace(table, ncap, body)) {
    idxDisable(g)
    return
  }
  *(g + gcIndexLive) = *(g + gcIndexLive) + 1
  *(g + gcIndexUsed) = *(g + gcIndexUsed) + 1
}

// Remove `body` from the index, leaving a tombstone so probe chains through
// this slot keep working. Tombstones are reclaimed by the next `idxGrow`.
@nosplit fn idxRemove(g: *i64, body: int) {
  if (*(g + gcIndexOk) == 0) {
    return
  }
  let ncap = *(g + gcIndexCap)
  if (ncap == 0) {
    return
  }
  let table = *(g + gcIndexAddr)
  let i = idxHash(body, ncap)
  let probes = 0
  while (probes < ncap) {
    let slotAddr = idxSlotAddr(table, i)
    let v = loadWord(slotAddr)
    if (v == idxEmpty) {
      return
    }
    if (v == body) {
      storeWord(slotAddr, idxTombstone)
      *(g + gcIndexLive) = *(g + gcIndexLive) - 1
      return
    }
    i = (i + 1) % ncap
    probes = probes + 1
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// #3147/#3177: WHY `gcOwns` STILL DOES NOT READ THE BITMAP — three findings
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// #3177 built the reader described above (mask to the span, find the span,
// read the class and the occupied-slot bit) and it is REVERTED. The ANSWER was
// right: an enumerated 9832-address differential against `gcOwnsLinear`
// (`tests/stress/gcslotbitmap`) reported 0 mismatches, and a shadow-mode build
// that computed both answers on `bench/cases/json` disagreed 0 times. What
// defeated it was everything around the answer, and all three findings are
// recorded here because each one will bite the next attempt.
//
// 1. THE MASK CANNOT DECIDE SPAN-BACKED-NESS, AND NOTHING ELSE IN THE RUNTIME
// CAN EITHER. `gcOwns` takes ARBITRARY integers: the conservative stack scan
// (ABI.md §5) pushes every raw machine word of a parked frame through
// `gcMarkConservative`, and `gcMarkRoot`'s `[gcLoAddr, gcHiAddr)` gate is a
// body-address hull, not a map of what is mapped — reclaimed spans are
// `munmap`ped inside it (#2375), large blocks are separate mappings, and both
// reserves' chunks sit between them. So masking to a span base and
// DEREFERENCING it is a wild read in the collector. There is no O(1) structure
// today that answers "is this address inside a currently-mapped span": the
// reserve chunk list is O(chunks) (99 on `json`), and per-class span lists are
// O(spans). A correct reader needs a real span-presence structure keyed on the
// MAPPING's lifetime, which needs stored words — and `gcWords` (38) and
// `heapWords` (41) are both full and boot-pinned.
//
// 2. A SPAN BASE IS A VALUE A LIVE OBJECT BODY CAN TAKE, so reusing this table
// for presence keys must TAG them. "No body inside a span equals its base" is
// true and is the wrong question: a body inside span S can equal the base of
// span S+1. Solve `spanSkip(cs)*cs + k*cs + gcHeaderSize == allocSpanBytes` and
// classes 0, 1 and 2 each have an in-range solution — class 0 at slot 16114 of
// 16118, class 1 at 8121 of 8123, class 2 at 4076 of 4077. Untagged, the
// presence probe hits those bodies and dereferences the NEXT span, which may be
// unmapped. `key = base + 1` separates the populations by residue mod 8 (bodies
// are `slotBase + gcHeaderSize`, both multiples of 8) and costs nothing.
//
// 3. THE MARK PATH HAS A STACK BUDGET, AND `bench/cases/json` IS ALREADY AT IT.
// A task stack is `schedStackBytes` = 65536 bytes, fixed, non-growable, and the
// task descriptor is found by MASKING the stack pointer to that boundary
// (`runtime/sched/scratch.bit`, `runtime/sched/sched.bit`) — so an overflow does
// not fault, it silently resolves to ANOTHER TASK's descriptor. The collector
// runs at a safepoint on top of whatever user code was running, and json's
// recursive-descent DOM parser is the deepest such stack in the bench suite.
// The reverted reader added ~224 bytes to the deepest mark chain (`slotBitSet`
// 0x50 + `allocSlotIndex` 0x60 + `allocSpanSlots` 0x30, measured with objdump on
// `runtime_gc.o`/`runtime_alloc.o`) and json went from 12/12 clean on `main` to
// crashing about half of all runs — with the ANSWER held at `main`'s, which is
// what proves it is the frames and not the logic. Raising `schedStackBytes` to
// 262144 as a diagnostic took it back to 7/8. Growing `idxGrow`'s 0x180 frame
// into `gcHeapAlloc` (an earlier placement of the presence insert) reproduced
// the same crash on the ALLOCATION path for the same reason.
//
// So the next attempt needs the stack budget addressed first, or a reader that
// adds no frames at all — and it must not buy that by copying
// `runtime/alloc/`'s span-layout arithmetic into this module, which is the
// duplication `bitlang-ws/CLAUDE.md` records as having caused divergent safety
// in `scripts/` ("a fix applied to a copy does not reach its siblings").

